title: Two outputs
mode: vhdl
chapter: Signals and gates
check: behaves
---- intro
A circuit can have as many outputs as you like. Each one gets its own line, and they can read the same input: one wire feeding two places.

Here there is one input `a` and two outputs, `y1` and `y2`. Both should follow `a`.
---- task
Connect both `y1` and `y2` to `a`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity fanout is
  port(
    a  : in  std_logic;
    y1 : out std_logic;
    y2 : out std_logic
  );
end fanout;

architecture rtl of fanout is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity fanout is
  port(
    a  : in  std_logic;
    y1 : out std_logic;
    y2 : out std_logic
  );
end fanout;

architecture rtl of fanout is
begin
    y1 <= a;
    y2 <= a;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Two lines, each like `y <= a;`.
---- hint2
`y1 <= a;` and `y2 <= a;`
---- solution
`y1 <= a;` and `y2 <= a;`. Click `a`: both lights follow it.
---- done
One input, read twice. In a real chip one gate's output often feeds dozens of others; engineers call it **fan-out**.
